course: dictionary
lesson: 1801
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one the writer means. The word clot is a good example. In «Blood clots to stop a cut from bleeding», clot means turning thick and forming lumps.
explain: The clue is the topic. When the sentence is about blood, a cut or a wound, clot is about blood going thick and sticky. When someone says «you clot!» with a smile, the word means a silly, clumsy person.
explain: Check by swapping in your meaning. Try «a silly person» in «the blood needs firm pressure so it can clot»: it does not fit. Try «form a thick lump» and it does, so that is the right meaning.
fact: A clot is a thick, sticky lump that forms in a liquid, especially in blood.
fact: To clot means to become thick and form lumps.
fact: «Clot» can be a noun or a verb, so check the sentence to see which one it is.
fact: In British English, «you clot!» is a mild, friendly way to call someone silly.
fact: A clot inside the body can be dangerous, so it needs a doctor.
exercise: «When you cut yourself, the blood forms a clot, which dries into a scab.» What is a clot here? | A silly, clumsy person | *A thick, sticky lump | A cloth for pressing on a cut | A kind of bat
exercise: «You clot!» Dad laughs when Sam drops the cake. What does he mean? | You have a cut on your hand | You are bleeding | *You silly person | You are very tired
exercise: «The cut stopped bleeding once the blood began to clot.» What does clot mean here? | *To become thick and form lumps | To laugh loudly | To run very fast | To grow a new hair
test: «A cut that bleeds a lot needs firm pressure, so the blood can clot and plug the hole.» What does clot mean here? | To wash the cloth | To bleed faster | To go cold | *To become thick and form a plug
test: Which word in this lesson means a thick, sticky lump? | cut | cloth | *clot | clean
exam: «Drugs like EPO thicken the blood, which can raise the risk of dangerous clots.» In this sentence, what are clots? | Cloths for cleaning | Cuts that need a plaster | *Thick, sticky lumps that can be dangerous | Kinds of drug
